     "reply": "Whether Mao Zedong did more harm than good remains a subject of intense historical and political debate, making a simple \"Yes\" or \"No\" insufficient to capture his legacy. \n\n* **The case that he did more harm** centers on the massive human cost of his policies. The Great Leap Forward (1958\u20131962) caused a catastrophic man-made famine that resulted in the deaths of an estimated 15 to 45 million people. Later, the Cultural Revolution (1966\u20131976) led to widespread persecution, the destruction of cultural heritage, social chaos, and the deaths of hundreds of thousands to millions more, while severely damaging the country's economy and intellectual life.\n\n* **The case that he did more good** emphasizes China's unification and sovereignty. Proponents credit Mao with ending a century of civil conflict and foreign domination (the \"Century of Humiliation\"), redistributing land to the peasantry, and establishing foundational public health and basic education systems that significantly increased life expectancy and literacy rates over his tenure.\n\nWithin China, the official Communist Party assessment delivered in 1981 characterized his legacy as \"70 percent good, 30 percent bad,\" while many independent historians outside China argue that the scale of human suffering under his rule heavily outweighs those achievements.",
